{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "id": "pw8-dune-sietch-drone",
    "name": "SIETCH DRONE",
    "author": "Patchwork Eight Engineering",
    "description": "Dune-film-score-aesthetic patch: a massive, dark, sub-heavy drone/bass built for scale and desolation, not melody. MASSIVE via an 8-operator stack spanning two octaves below root plus a detuned unison cluster at the root and a stacked open 5th (the same open-interval language Zimmer's Dune score leans on for 'vast desert' scale, via real detuned-oscillator technique, not a quotation of any cue). DARK via Filter 1 lowpass at 380Hz with modest resonance. EVOLVING via LFO1 -> FilterCutoff (very slow sweep, breathing room tone) and Velocity -> FilterCutoff (hit-hard brightens), plus the redesigned Bricasti-M7-informed Reverb's own frequency-dependent decay (low end rings far longer than highs) and internal late-tank modulation, and TapeDelay's wow/flutter drift for a second, independent layer of slow movement. A rendered preview of this patch (parameter-surface-only, since mod routes below aren't reachable via a plain VST3 parameter list) is at scratchpad/dune/sietch_drone.wav / the published 'Dune Session' artifact -- the mod routes here make the real loaded patch noticeably more alive than that preview.",
    "category": "pad",
    "moods": ["dark", "massive", "desolate", "evolving", "cinematic"],
    "genres": ["score", "ambient"],
    "tags": ["dune", "drone", "sub-bass", "reverb-showcase"],
    "createdAt": "2026-08-10T00:00:00Z",
